# Scope: Adaptive System

This segment names AAT's broadest scope. A system is an **adaptive system** at time $t$ when its world can still show it something it does not yet know, either about *where things are* (the environment's state $\Omega_t$) or about *how things work* (the environment's law $\theta$: the rules by which the state moves and is observed). Part I's machinery (mismatch, gain, tempo, persistence) is the theory of correcting a model against what the world shows, so this is the condition under which there is something to correct toward.

Two familiar systems sit inside for opposite reasons. A Kalman filter tracking a moving signal knows the law exactly and is uncertain about the state. A tabular reinforcement learner reads its state exactly and is uncertain about the law, because it does not know the transition probabilities. Passive Bayesian learners and biological sensory systems are inside as well; none of these needs to *act* on its environment for Part I to apply.

Two kinds of system sit outside. The first already has, in its history and its design, everything its world could reveal: a fully observed process whose transition probabilities it was given, deterministic or stochastic. Nothing about that world remains to be learned; what remains is planning under chance. The second has an observation channel that carries nothing about state or law, which includes having no channel at all. Limited computing power never brings a system inside: an agent that has everything the world could reveal but cannot work out the consequences fast enough is outside, and its errors, which are its own, are priced by tempo. Nor does it put a system outside: a slow Kalman filter is still tracking a hidden state.

The scope classifies situations, not competent adapters: a system is inside when its world has something left to show it, whether or not it uses what it is shown well. And the classification rests on two declarations, which this segment makes explicit because no observation can make them. The first is *what the world is*: which variables are its state and which its law. Every loop has a re-description that produces exactly the same observations and is outside, so membership belongs to the world as declared, not to the agent's experience (the housing result below). AAT declares the physical world. The second is *what the agent was given*: the reference measure over possible worlds is fixed by what the agent knows by design, not by what its designer knows or what it believes.

## Formal Expression

*[Definition (environment-law; typed-epistemic-target)]*

The environment's **law** is $\theta = (\theta_T, \theta_h)$: the transition kernel $T$ of #def-action-transition and the observation kernel $p_h(\cdot \mid \Omega_t, a_{t-1})$, the conditional law of $o_t$ that $h$ and $\varepsilon_t$ jointly induce ( #def-observation-function). Whatever part of the law the analysis does not fix is random under a reference measure $\pi_0$ on $(\Omega_1, \theta)$: the law of the world given the information the agent holds by design (its knowledge, as opposed to its beliefs). A Kalman filter built with its model has a point mass on the law; a tabular learner not given its transition probabilities has a non-degenerate measure over them; a false belief is not information and does not shrink $\pi_0$. By item (h) below, only the support of $\pi_0$ affects membership. The **typed epistemic target** at time $t$ is

$$S_t = (\Omega_t, \theta).$$

The cut between the components is **invariance**. Whatever about the world stays the same along the whole run is named into $\theta$, and whatever varies, however slowly, is state. A drifting regime is therefore slow state, and $\theta$ holds the law of its drift. Given $\theta$, $\Omega_t$ is sufficient for its own evolution (the Markov commitment of #def-action-transition, read with the law held fixed). When the law is fully known, $\pi_0$ is a point mass in $\theta$ and $S_t$ carries no more uncertainty than $\Omega_t$.

*[Scope (scope-adaptive-system)]*

Let $F_t = (a_t, o_{t+1}, a_{t+1}, o_{t+2}, \ldots)$ be the loop's future stream after $t$, and let $\pi'$ range over the ways of continuing the agent's actions from $t$ on: any policy over $\mathcal A$ that may depend on the history and on independent randomness, with its actions recorded in the chronica ( #def-chronica). The loop is in the **adaptive scope at time $t$** if

$$\mathcal{S}_\text{adaptive}(t) = \left\{(\text{Agent}, \Omega) \;:\; \mathcal{O} \neq \emptyset, \;\; \sup_{\pi'} \, I_{\pi'}\big(S_t \,;\, F_t \,\big\vert\, \mathcal C_t\big) \gt 0 \right\}$$

with $\Omega$ read as the declared world: its state process, its law, and $\pi_0$. Two conditions:

1. **The world can still reveal something about state or law**: under some continuation, the future stream carries information about $S_t$ beyond the history.

2. **A perceptual channel exists**: $\mathcal{O} \neq \emptyset$ ( #def-observation-function). This restates part of condition 1 rather than adding to it (with no channel, the future stream holds only actions, which carry nothing about $S_t$ beyond the history); it is kept as the visible gate.

The mutual information averages over histories; on a single run, condition on the realized history $\mathcal C_t = c_t$ instead. The predicate is dated, so membership is assessed at each $t$, and a result that cites this scope uses it at the times the result is applied.

*[Derived (adaptive-scope-membership), exact]* Each item is a short computation from the definitions above.

- **(a) Residual uncertainty is necessary, not sufficient.** If the conditional law of $S_t$ given $\mathcal C_t$ is a point mass, every mutual information with $S_t$ is zero, so condition 1 fails. Membership therefore requires residual uncertainty about the typed target, written $H(S_t \mid \mathcal C_t) \gt 0$ in the discrete case (for continuous targets, read it as "the conditional law is not a point mass"). Items (b), (e) and (f) show residual uncertainty that does not suffice.

- **(b) Hidden state, known law (a Kalman filter).** Take $x_{t+1} = A x_t + w_t$ and $o_t = C x_t + v_t$ with $A, C$ and the noise covariances known, so $\theta$ is fixed and $S_t = x_t$ with posterior $\mathcal N(\hat x_t, P_t)$. Each later observation is $o_{t+j} = C A^j x_t$ plus noise terms independent of $(x_t, \mathcal C_t)$, so the two are jointly Gaussian given $\mathcal C_t$ with cross-covariance $P_t (A^j)^\top C^\top$. Hence $I(x_t; F_t \mid \mathcal C_t) \gt 0$ if and only if $C A^j P_t \neq 0$ for some $j \geq 1$. The filter is in exactly when the uncertain state persists into what is later observed. The random-walk tracker of #example-kalman is in at every $t$ (under sensor choice, membership needs one continuation, and always using the better sensor is Gaussian), and stays in because $P_t$ converges to a positive steady state. A state drawn afresh at every step ($A = 0$) is out: nothing seen later bears on it.

- **(c) Observed state, unknown law (tabular reinforcement learning).** Take $o_t = \Omega_t$ on a finite state space, with the rows $T(\cdot \mid s, a)$ unknown under independent Dirichlet priors. The state is read off the history, so all the information is about $\theta_T$. At the current state $s$, taking action $a$ makes the next observation a draw from $T(\cdot \mid s, a)$, whose posterior is not a point mass after finitely many visits. The information the draw carries about the row is the entropy of the predictive (the posterior-mean row) minus the expected entropy of the row, which is positive by strict concavity of entropy whenever the row is not a.s. constant and has at least two possible successors. So the learner is in at every finite $t$, and never leaves; what decays, roughly as $1/(2n)$ after $n$ visits to a pair, is the amount of information. Under a different prior, one that puts the rows on deterministic successor maps, an agent that has visited every reachable pair knows its law exactly and leaves the scope, having become a planner.

- **(d) Observed state, known law.** $S_t$ is determined by $\mathcal C_t$, so condition 1 fails: out, whether the world is deterministic or stochastic.

- **(e) A channel that carries nothing.** If, under every continuation, the future observations are independent of $S_t$ given the history (a constant channel, or one that emits noise whose law is known and independent of the state), condition 1 fails: out. The empty channel is the extreme case. A noise channel whose law the agent was not given is different: its observations carry information about $\theta_h$, and the loop is in, as a passive learner of its own noise.

- **(f) Unreachable uncertainty.** Uncertainty confined to coordinates of $S_t$ on which no continuation's observations depend, such as the transition law at state–action pairs that no policy can reach, does not count toward membership.

- **(g) Leaving the law part is final.** Because $\theta$ is invariant, $I_{\pi'}(\theta; F_t \mid \mathcal C_t) = I_{\pi'}(\theta; a_t, o_{t+1}, \ldots, o_s \mid \mathcal C_t) + I_{\pi'}(\theta; F_s \mid \mathcal C_s)$ for $s \gt t$ by the chain rule. The agent's conduct from $t$ to $s$ followed by any continuation from $s$ is itself a continuation from $t$, so if no continuation from $t$ reveals anything about $\theta$, none from any later time does, for almost every history reached by $s$. The state part has no such property: a state that keeps moving keeps renewing what can be revealed about it.

- **(h) Only the possible worlds matter.** Given $(S_t, \mathcal C_t)$, the future stream is independent of $\Omega_1$ (the Markov commitment, with actions functions of the history and independent randomness). Replacing $\pi_0$ by a measure with a positive density on $(\Omega_1, \theta)$ therefore reweights only the law of $(S_t, \mathcal C_t)$ and leaves the conditional law of $F_t$ given $(S_t, \mathcal C_t)$ unchanged. Conditional independence of $S_t$ and $F_t$ given $\mathcal C_t$ is preserved, so membership is the same under any prior with the same null sets as $\pi_0$.

*[Derived (adaptive-scope-housing), exact]* **Membership is a property of the declared world, not of the interaction law.** Take any loop and re-describe its world. Let the new state $\Omega'_t$ be the pair (Bayes posterior over $S_t$ given $\mathcal C_t$ under $\pi_0$, latest observation), let the new world emit $o_{t+1}$ from the Bayes predictive $p(o_{t+1} \mid \mathcal C_t, a_t)$, and let it update the posterior by Bayes' rule. When the law is known this is the belief-state reduction of a partially observed process (Åström 1965; Kaelbling, Littman & Cassandra 1998). When the law is unknown it is the Bayes-adaptive reduction: for tabular reinforcement learning the new state is the current state together with the transition counts (Martin 1967; Duff 2002). Four facts follow:

- the new world is Markov;

- its kernel is one fixed function of state and action, because the Bayes posterior given a history does not depend on the policy that produced it;

- its channel reads the stored observation without noise;

- it gives every action-observation sequence the same probability as the original world does, under every policy, because the two agree on the one-step predictive given any history.

But $\Omega'_t$ is a function of $\mathcal C_t$ and its law is known, so the re-described loop is a case-(d) loop and condition 1 fails: it is out, while the original may be in. Every loop therefore has an observationally identical twin that is out, and a condition that depends only on the interaction law cannot both include anything and exclude every case-(d) loop. Conditions stated on the declared target, including $H(S_t \mid \mathcal C_t) \gt 0$ and the state-only predicate it replaces, can draw the line, but only relative to the declaration. AAT reads the world as the declared physical world ( #def-agent-environment): its physical state and its physical law, each known or open as the agent's design makes it. Under that declaration, both known-law tracking and law learning are in.

## Epistemic Status

*Axiomatic* for the scope itself. The definition of the typed target, the invariance cut, and the predicate are not derived; they are the condition under which the world has something left to show the agent. The rule fixing $\pi_0$ by the agent's design-time knowledge is likewise definitional. The membership consequences (a)–(h) and the housing result are *exact*: each is a short computation from the definitions, given above. The Kalman criterion in (b) is standard Gaussian conditioning, and the housing result rests on the classical belief-state and Bayes-adaptive reductions. Not claimed: that the cut between $\Omega_t$ and $\theta$, or the choice of $\Omega$, can be read off the observations. The housing result shows it cannot, and the declaration is the modeler's. What the conditions supply is non-vacuity: they make mismatch, gain, tempo and persistence statements about adapting to a world rather than about chance or the agent's own errors. They do not by themselves deliver Part I's results, which carry their own assumptions (the global assumptions in NOTATION.md).

## Discussion

**Two kinds of warrant.** A system can be in scope because the state is unknown, or because the law is unknown, and the two behave differently in time. Uncertainty about a moving state is renewed every step, so a Kalman filter's warrant is *standing*: the information stays bounded away from zero while the observed part of the signal moves. Uncertainty about an invariant law is spent as it is learned, so a learner's warrant is *decaying*: the information tends to zero, though under a continuous prior it stays positive at every finite time. By (g), if it ever reaches zero it stays there; that happens in finite time only when the law can be identified exactly in finite time, and then the learner of a fixed world has become a planner. A drifting law is slow state, which is why the realistic case, where the rules themselves shift, keeps a standing warrant.

**The collapse map.** Each kind of knowledge removes one kind of work. A known state leaves nothing to *filter*; a known law leaves nothing to *learn*; both together leave nothing to *adapt* to, which is case (d). The four ways to be behind, and their remedies, follow the same carve:

| Behind on | Example | Remedy | In this scope? |

| --- | --- | --- | --- |

| state: where things are | where the lane edge is right now | observe, probe | yes |

| law: how things work | how this car brakes on this ice | accumulate experience, intervene | yes |

| chance: what is not yet decided | the next gust | accept it, or change the channel | no; it is what remains when state and law are known |

| speed: knowing enough but not computing in time | the skid you could compute, but not before the curve | think faster, or act earlier | no; priced by tempo ( #def-adaptive-tempo, #der-deliberation-cost) |

**What the old exclusion got right and wrong.** "Full knowledge of the environment" correctly excludes a world with nothing left to learn, but full knowledge means knowledge of the state *and* the law. Reading the state exactly is not enough: a learner that sees its state perfectly and does not know the transition probabilities faces live prediction error, which is precisely the setting of tabular reinforcement learning. Item (c) puts it inside. Item (d) keeps the true core of the exclusion, a known world fully observed, which is planning under chance rather than adaptation.

**Why the future stream, not residual entropy.** Residual uncertainty about $S_t$ is necessary (item (a)) but counts uncertainty the world can never show. It counts a channel that carries nothing (e), law at unreachable pairs (f), and a state drawn afresh each step and seen through noise (b with $A = 0$). The last case is indistinguishable from a world with no state and more channel noise: re-describing it that way changes no observable and flips the entropy verdict, while the future-stream verdict is "out" both ways. The mutual information is also well defined for continuous states and kernel-valued laws, where a differential entropy would depend on units or need a reference measure. The quantity is the retrospective, whole-future counterpart of the state-and-law information term (ii′) in the information-form mismatch decomposition of #deriv-mismatch-budget-attribution. Term (ii′) for $o_{t+1}$ measures what that observation says about the target it is emitted from, $S_{t+1}$; the predicate's one-step part measures what it says about the target now, $S_t$, and by data processing is at most the first. They are positive together when the target persists; with a state drawn afresh ($A = 0$), (ii′) is positive and the predicate is zero, so the mismatch decomposition books a positive floor for a loop outside the scope. That floor's label is the declaration-relative one (see #result-mismatch-decomposition's Epistemic Status).

**Whose uncertainty.** The predicate is computed by the modeler, over the true $S_t$, but under $\pi_0$, the law of the world given what the agent knows by design. Two jobs that "the modeler's viewpoint" would run together are thereby separated: whose probability (the modeler's), and whose knowledge (the agent's). An agent that is certain it has its world figured out, and is wrong, is still in scope, because a false belief is not knowledge, and it is about to be surprised. A benchmark whose transition law its designer knows stays in scope for the learner that was not given it, and a planner handed its true law is out. By (h), $\pi_0$ matters only through which worlds it allows. The chronica does not record what the agent was built knowing, so this rule is part of the declaration, not something the history settles. The predicate is also evaluated over every continuation, not only the agent's own conduct. An agent whose own policy reveals nothing it does not already know, while some other policy would, is in scope; that is a fact about the agent's conduct, not about the world (the absorbing regions of #hyp-solicitable-escape are the case where such conduct locks in).

**The declared world.** The housing result says that whether an agent is tracking a hidden world, learning an unknown law, or watching a known random process cannot be settled from the agent's data. Nothing in AAT is lost by saying so: the result is the same in kind as the observation that fated and genuine randomness cannot be told apart from inside ( #disc-law-discovery-ceiling), and #def-agent-environment already treats the agent–environment decomposition as a modeling choice. What the declaration buys is that Part I's persistence race, keeping up with a world that moves, is a statement about the physical state the modeler names. The magnitude of the mismatch an agent experiences does not depend on the declaration; its attribution to a moving world, as against irreducible noise, does.

**Narrowing to agency.** Adding causal action unlocks the interventional and purposeful results of Parts II and III. The agency scope ( #scope-agency) is the intersection of $\mathcal{S}_\text{adaptive}$ with the condition that actions carry Pearl-level-2 contrast: distinct actions produce distinct interventional outcome distributions. Adaptive-scope systems that remain outside agency are *passive observers* (no choice) or *nominal agents* (choices with no causal effect). For both, Part I's machinery applies but the causal-information and purposeful-agent results do not.
